The wcd937x reset pin hangs off the PMIC over SPMI, which means that writing it
SLEEPS. The driver uses gpiod_set_value(), which is the variant for pins that
do not sleep, and the kernel warns:

    WARNING: gpiod_set_value
    lr : wcd937x_reset+0x28/0x68 [snd_soc_wcd937x]
         wcd937x_reset+0x44/0x68 [snd_soc_wcd937x]
         wcd937x_probe+0x264/0x2f0 [snd_soc_wcd937x]

The two offsets in the warning are exactly the two calls here.

It matters more than an ugly warning. The reset is a pulse with two waits of
20-30 us:

    raise the pin ; usleep_range(20,30) ; lower the pin ; usleep_range(20,30)

If each write of the pin travels over a slow bus of variable duration, the
pulse does not last what the driver believes. A badly applied reset leaves the
codec without coming out of its initial state, and right afterwards this
appears in the log:

    qcom-soundwire 62ef0000.soundwire: qcom_swrm_irq_handler: SWR bus clsh detected

which is a collision on the SoundWire bus. It is the last thing the phone says
before freezing in the boots that fail (~25%). See
surya/docs/BOOT-01-not-a-hang.md.

gpiod_set_value_cansleep() is the correct variant for a pin that sleeps, and it
is what the rest of the drivers in the same situation use.

CAREFUL: this is NOT proven to be the cause. It is the candidate that comes out
of the evidence. It is measured with surya/arranque/bucle-reinicios, 20 cycles
with the audio starting up in full, against the failure rate without the patch.

--- a/sound/soc/codecs/wcd937x.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/wcd937x.c
@@ -239,9 +239,9 @@

 static void wcd937x_reset(struct wcd937x_priv *wcd937x)
 {
-	gpiod_set_value(wcd937x->reset_gpio, 1);
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(wcd937x->reset_gpio, 1);
 	usleep_range(20, 30);
-	gpiod_set_value(wcd937x->reset_gpio, 0);
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(wcd937x->reset_gpio, 0);
 	usleep_range(20, 30);
 }

